# Interface files

When many people or agents change one codebase, a change to a module's public
interface is the signal that other code is affected. `mylang api` makes that
interface a small generated text file checked in beside each module source, so
a reviewer sees an interface change as an ordinary diff, and an edit that only
touches bodies never touches the file.

## `mylang api`

Every project module gets one file beside its source, with `.api` in place of
`.my`: `shop/billing.my` gets `shop/billing.api`. Embedded standard modules get
none.

```console
$ mylang api examples/interface-files/main.my --write
api: wrote shop/money.api (shop.money)
api: wrote shop/billing.api (shop.billing)
api: wrote main.api (main)
api: 3 interface files current (3 written, 0 deleted)
$ mylang api examples/interface-files/main.my --check
api: 3 interface files current
```

| Option | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `--write` | Write every missing or stale file, leave current ones untouched, and delete orphaned ones. |
| `--check` | Write nothing. Exit 1 when any file is missing, stale or orphaned, and show what changed. |
| `--json` | Print one `interface-files-v1` JSON object instead of the text report. |

Exactly one of `--write` and `--check` is required.

| Exit | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| 0 | Every file is current (`--check`), or the files were written (`--write`) |
| 1 | `--check` found a missing, stale or orphaned file; or a declaration does not resolve |
| 2 | Usage error, or an interface file that cannot be read, written or deleted |

The command reads declarations only. Bodies are never checked, so it runs in
the middle of a change just as `list-interfaces` does.

## What the file contains

Here is `examples/interface-files/shop/billing.api`, generated from
`shop/billing.my`:

```console
$ cat examples/interface-files/shop/billing.api
# generated by mylang api; do not edit
module shop.billing
"""Invoices and their totals."""

public const TAX_PERCENT: int = 8

public record Line:
    quantity: int
    unit_cents: int

@ensures(result >= 0)
public def total(lines: list[Line], *, taxed: bool = True) -> int ceiling(managed_allocation)

public def charge(lines: list[Line]) -> shop.money.Cents ceiling(managed_allocation)
```

The file is mylang declaration syntax without bodies:

- the header line, the `module` line and the first paragraph of the module
  docstring;
- every public declaration, in source order, one block each: records, classes,
  unions, enums and results with their fields and cases; domains with their
  codec and their public members nested beneath them; resources; type aliases;
  named constraints; `const` declarations with their type and value;
  re-exports as `export module.Name`;
- each public function's whole signature: type parameters and their
  requirements, `mut` markers, the keyword-only `*`, defaults, the return type,
  any `noalloc`, `noio` or `effects(...)` clause as written, and the
  `ceiling(...)` that `list-interfaces` publishes (the effects the parameters
  permit, narrowed by that clause);
- the `@deprecated`, `@requires`, `@ensures` and `@invariant` clauses above a
  declaration, as written, with runs of whitespace folded to one space
  ([contracts](../contracts.md#reports-interfaces-and-caches)).

Types are written canonically. Aliases are expanded, capabilities use their
Python spelling (`Stream`, `Filesystem`), the module's own declarations are
unqualified, and every other declaration is qualified by its full module name
(`shop.money.Cents`, not the local import alias).

Everything the file shows comes from the declaration index. Nothing is inferred
from a function body. The file stays byte-identical when an edit changes only:

- a function body;
- a private declaration;
- a comment or a declaration's own docstring;
- the layout of a signature.

The same sources always render the same bytes, whether the cache is cold, warm
or disabled.

## Checking and reviewing a change

`--check` lists each out-of-date file with a unified diff from the checked-in
file to the regenerated one (at most 40 lines per module), then the command to
run:

```console
$ mylang api main.my --check
api: app is stale (app.api)
--- app.api
+++ app.api (generated)
@@ -2,3 +2,3 @@
 module app
 
-public def compute(value: int) -> int ceiling(managed_allocation)
+public def compute(value: int, extra: int = 1) -> int ceiling(managed_allocation)
api: 1 interface files out of date; run `mylang api main.my --write`
$ mylang api main.my --check --json
{"version":"interface-files-v1","mode":"check","status":"stale","files":2,"modules":[{"module":"app","path":"app.api","state":"stale","added":[],"removed":[],"changed":["compute"]}]}
```

In the JSON report, `state` is `missing`, `stale` or `orphaned`. The `added`,
`removed` and `changed` lists name declarations: a domain member is
`Domain.member`, and the module docstring is `__doc__`. An orchestrator can read
them to see which callers a change may affect.

An orphan is a `.api` file under the entry's directory (hidden directories
and `zig-out` are not searched) whose `.my` source no longer exists and that
still starts with the generated header. `--write` deletes
it. Two kinds of file are never touched: one without the header (written by
hand), and one whose source still exists but is not loaded from this entry,
because it may belong to another entry's graph.

## Enforcing current files with `mylang.json`

A project opts in by setting `interface_files` in its
[`mylang.json`](import-policy.md#configuration-shape):

```json
{"version": 1, "interface_files": true}
```

Then, once a project's bodies check, `check`, `check-project`, `run`,
`run-project` and `build` reject a missing or stale interface file:

```console
$ mylang check main.my
app.api:1:1: error[E_INTERFACE_FILE_STALE]: the interface file of module `app` is stale; run `mylang api main.my --write` and review its diff
```

The key must be `true` or `false`, and leaving it out means `false`: nothing
changes for a project that does not set it. The bytes of every checked-in
interface file are part of the configuration identity that the verdict caches
use, so editing or deleting one never replays an earlier successful check.
Navigation and report commands (`index-project`, `context`, `list-interfaces`,
`lint`) do not enforce the files; `mylang test` checks the project the way
`check` does, so a stale file fails a test run too, and such a project's test
runs never replay a whole recorded report.
`examples/interface-files` is a project with the key set and its files checked
in.

## Relation to `api-diff`

[`mylang api-diff`](api-diff.md) classifies a change against a recorded
baseline of declaration fingerprints. Interface files are that baseline as
readable text, kept in version control. Both show only facts that belong to a
declaration's `interface_sha256`. The one addition is the module docstring's
first paragraph, which the interface files include and the fingerprint does
not.

//! Generated interface files: canonical content, stability under body-only
//! edits, sensitivity to interface edits, declaration-level comparison, the
//! unified diff, and the `mylang api` write/check/orphan cycle on disk.
const std = @import("std");
const t = std.testing;
const graph = @import("project.zig");
const interfaces = @import("interfaces.zig");
const interface_files = @import("interface_files.zig");
const api_files = @import("api_files.zig");

const shapes_path = "pkg/shapes.my";
const shapes_module = "pkg.shapes";

const File = struct { path: []const u8, bytes: []const u8 };
const Files = struct {
    entries: []const File,
    fn read(context: ?*anyopaque, allocator: std.mem.Allocator, path: []const u8, limit: usize) graph.ReadError![]u8 {
        const self: *Files = @ptrCast(@alignCast(context.?));
        for (self.entries) |file| if (std.mem.eql(u8, file.path, path)) {
            if (file.bytes.len > limit) return error.TooLarge;
            return allocator.dupe(u8, file.bytes);
        };
        return error.NotFound;
    }
    fn provider(self: *Files) graph.Provider {
        return .{ .context = self, .read = read };
    }
};

const entry =
    \\module main
    \\import pkg.shapes
    \\
    \\def main() -> int:
    \\    return 0
    \\
;

const units =
    \\module pkg.units
    \\
    \\public domain Metres: int:
    \\    private def raw(value: int) -> Metres:
    \\        return wrap[Metres](value)
    \\
    \\    @requires(value >= 0)
    \\    def of(value: int) -> Metres effects():
    \\        return Metres.raw(value)
    \\
    \\    def value(m: Metres) -> int effects():
    \\        return unwrap(m)
    \\
;

const shapes =
    \\module pkg.shapes
    \\import pkg.units
    \\"""Shapes and their areas.
    \\
    \\Only this first paragraph is published.
    \\"""
    \\
    \\export units.Metres
    \\
    \\
    \\public const SIDES: int = 4
    \\
    \\
    \\# A comment the interface never shows.
    \\@invariant(self.width >= 0)
    \\public record Rect:
    \\    width: int
    \\    height: int
    \\
    \\
    \\public class Canvas[T: copy]:
    \\    items: list[T]
    \\
    \\
    \\public union Shape:
    \\    empty,
    \\    square: int,
    \\    rect(width: int, height: int)
    \\
    \\
    \\public result Parsed:
    \\    ok: Rect,
    \\    err: str
    \\
    \\
    \\public type Scale = def(int) -> int effects()
    \\
    \\
    \\public constraint Sized[S] = copy + field(width: int)
    \\
    \\
    \\@requires(rect.width >= 0,
    \\          "width is never negative")
    \\@ensures(result >= 0)
    \\public def area(rect: Rect, *, scale: int = 1) -> int noalloc:
    \\    """The area, scaled."""
    \\    return rect.width * rect.height * scale
    \\
    \\
    \\public def draw(canvas: mut Canvas[int], out: Stream) -> int:
    \\    out.print("draw")
    \\    return 0
    \\
    \\
    \\public def stamp(fs: Filesystem, clock: Clock, env: Environment) -> int:
    \\    return 0
    \\
    \\
    \\public def widest[S: Sized](items: list[S]) -> int:
    \\    return 0
    \\
    \\
    \\def helper() -> int:
    \\    return 1
    \\
;

const shapes_interface =
    \\# generated by mylang api; do not edit
    \\module pkg.shapes
    \\"""Shapes and their areas."""
    \\
    \\export pkg.units.Metres
    \\
    \\public const SIDES: int = 4
    \\
    \\@invariant(self.width >= 0)
    \\public record Rect:
    \\    width: int
    \\    height: int
    \\
    \\public class Canvas[T: copy]:
    \\    items: list[T]
    \\
    \\public union Shape:
    \\    empty,
    \\    square: int,
    \\    rect(width: int, height: int)
    \\
    \\public result Parsed:
    \\    ok: Rect,
    \\    err: str
    \\
    \\public type Scale = def(int) -> int effects()
    \\
    \\public constraint Sized[S] = copy + field(width:int)
    \\
    \\@requires(rect.width >= 0, "width is never negative")
    \\@ensures(result >= 0)
    \\public def area(rect: Rect, *, scale: int = 1) -> int noalloc ceiling()
    \\
    \\public def draw(canvas: mut Canvas[int], out: Stream) -> int ceiling(managed_allocation,output,mutation)
    \\
    \\public def stamp(fs: Filesystem, clock: Clock, env: Environment) -> int ceiling(filesystem,clock,environment,managed_allocation)
    \\
    \\public def widest[S: Sized](items: list[S]) -> int ceiling(managed_allocation)
    \\
;

const units_interface =
    \\# generated by mylang api; do not edit
    \\module pkg.units
    \\
    \\public domain Metres: int:
    \\    @requires(value >= 0)
    \\    def of(value: int) -> Metres effects() ceiling()
    \\    def value(m: Metres) -> int effects() ceiling()
    \\
;

/// Render one module of the three-module fixture whose `pkg/shapes.my` is
/// `shapes_source`. The caller owns the text.
fn renderShapes(allocator: std.mem.Allocator, shapes_source: []const u8, module: []const u8) ![]u8 {
    var files: Files = .{ .entries = &.{
        .{ .path = "main.my", .bytes = entry },
        .{ .path = shapes_path, .bytes = shapes_source },
        .{ .path = "pkg/units.my", .bytes = units },
    } };
    var project = graph.Project.init(allocator);
    defer project.deinit();
    try project.load(files.provider(), "main", .{});
    var index = try interfaces.build(&project);
    return interface_files.render(allocator, &index, project.names.get(module).?);
}

/// Render `pkg.shapes` after replacing `from` with `to` in its source.
fn renderEdited(allocator: std.mem.Allocator, from: []const u8, to: []const u8) ![]u8 {
    try t.expect(std.mem.indexOf(u8, shapes, from) != null);
    const edited = try std.mem.replaceOwned(u8, allocator, shapes, from, to);
    defer allocator.free(edited);
    return renderShapes(allocator, edited, shapes_module);
}

// spec: API-FILE-1
test "an interface file renders every public declaration in source order without bodies" {
    const rendered = try renderShapes(t.allocator, shapes, shapes_module);
    defer t.allocator.free(rendered);
    try t.expectEqualStrings(shapes_interface, rendered);
    const domain = try renderShapes(t.allocator, shapes, "pkg.units");
    defer t.allocator.free(domain);
    try t.expectEqualStrings(units_interface, domain);
    try t.expect(interface_files.generated(rendered));
    try t.expectEqualStrings(shapes_module, interface_files.declaredModule(rendered).?);
}

// spec-case: API-FILE-1
test "an interface file is byte-identical after a body, private, comment or formatting edit" {
    const base = try renderShapes(t.allocator, shapes, shapes_module);
    defer t.allocator.free(base);
    const body = try renderEdited(t.allocator, "    return rect.width * rect.height * scale", "    total = rect.height * rect.width\n    return total * scale");
    defer t.allocator.free(body);
    try t.expectEqualStrings(base, body);
    const private = try renderEdited(t.allocator, "def helper() -> int:\n    return 1", "def helper(x: int) -> str:\n    return \"changed\"");
    defer t.alloca
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Run `mylang interfaces oracle` over every entry of a corpus and summarize.

usage: interface_oracle.py MYLANG ENTRY_OR_DIR... [--jobs N] [--json OUT]

A directory contributes every `*.my` file under it (recursively) as an entry.
Each entry's oracle checks every module of its project against its imports'
interfaces and compares the result with the whole-program check. The summary
counts entries, modules, functions and contexts compared, stand-ins, and every
mismatch; the exit status is 1 when any entry differs or fails to run.
"""
import argparse
import concurrent.futures
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import tempfile
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from pathlib import Path


def entries(paths):
    out = []
    for path in paths:
        p = Path(path)
        if p.is_dir():
            out += sorted(q for q in p.rglob("*.my") if ".mylang-cache" not in q.parts)
        else:
            out.append(p)
    return out


def run(mylang, entry):
    source = entry.read_text()
    temporary = None
    target = entry.resolve()
    if not re.search(r"^module\s", source, re.M):
        temporary = tempfile.TemporaryDirectory(prefix="interface-oracle-")
        mirror = Path(temporary.name) / "src"
        shutil.copytree(entry.parent, mirror, ignore=shutil.ignore_patterns(".mylang-cache"))
        target = mirror / entry.name
        target.write_text(f"module {entry.stem}; " + source)
    started = time.perf_counter()
    proc = subprocess.run([mylang, "interfaces", "oracle", str(target)], capture_output=True, text=True,
                          env=dict(os.environ))
    wall = time.perf_counter() - started
    if temporary is not None:
        temporary.cleanup()
    try:
        report = json.loads(proc.stdout)
    except json.JSONDecodeError:
        report = None
    return {"entry": str(entry), "status": proc.returncode, "wall": wall, "report": report, "stderr": proc.stderr[-2000:]}


def main():
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
    parser.add_argument("mylang")
    parser.add_argument("paths", nargs="+")
    parser.add_argument("--jobs", type=int, default=min(8, os.cpu_count() or 1))
    parser.add_argument("--json")
    args = parser.parse_args()
    todo = entries(args.paths)
    with concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=args.jobs) as pool:
        results = list(pool.map(lambda e: run(args.mylang, e), todo))
    totals = {"entries": len(results), "entries_equal": 0, "entries_not_checked": 0, "modules": 0, "functions": 0,
              "contexts": 0, "stood_in": 0, "whole_program_diagnostics": 0, "mismatching_entries": []}
    for result in results:
        report = result["report"]
        if report is None:
            totals["entries_not_checked"] += 1
            totals["mismatching_entries"].append({"entry": result["entry"], "status": result["status"], "stderr": result["stderr"]})
            continue
        totals["modules"] += report["modules"]
        totals["functions"] += report["functions_compared"]
        totals["contexts"] += report["contexts_compared"]
        totals["stood_in"] += report["stood_in"]
        totals["whole_program_diagnostics"] += report["whole_program_diagnostics"]
        if report["equal"]:
            totals["entries_equal"] += 1
            continue
        bad = [r for r in report["results"] if r["status"] != "checked" or any(r["mismatches"].values())]
        totals["mismatching_entries"].append({"entry": result["entry"], "missing": report["missing_diagnostics"], "modules": bad[:5]})
    if args.json:
        Path(args.json).write_text(json.dumps({"totals": totals, "results": results}, indent=1))
    print(json.dumps(totals, indent=1))
    return 0 if not totals["mismatching_entries"] else 1


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())

//! The `mylang` commands that build, run or test code, on the new core (S5-A,
//! NEW-CORE-S5-CLI-1): `build` (and `build --run`), `run`, `run-project`, `test`,
//! and the developer commands `dump-ir`, `dump-typed` and `coverage` (S5-D8). The
//! old back end's commands and flags are refused by name with what replaces them
//! (NEW-CORE-S5-RETIRED-1). `src/main.zig` asks `dispatch` first; null hands the
//! command to the front-end commands it still owns.
const std = @import("std");
const core_main = @import("main.zig");
const cli_args = @import("cli_args.zig");
const cli_run = @import("cli_run.zig");
const pipeline = @import("pipeline.zig");
const cost_command = @import("cost_command.zig");

/// What stops a command before it has a status: memory, an unwritable stream, or a
/// test the runner cannot encode.
pub const Error = core_main.Error || cost_command.Error;

/// A retired command or flag and the one-line reason the CLI gives.
const Retirement = struct { name: []const u8, message: []const u8 };

const qbe_emission = "QBE IL emission was retired with the old back end (S5); the compiler emits LLVM: use `mylang build FILE.my -o OUT --emit-llvm PATH`";
const host_retired = "host functions, libraries and providers were retired (S5-D5); use `extern` with `--link FILE.c` for C interop";
const runtime_retired = "runtime selection was retired (S5-D4): one-count reference counting is the only memory model; drop the flag";
const module_build_retired = "per-module QBE builds were retired (S5); `mylang build` compiles and caches one unit per module";

/// Commands the new core does not have, with what replaces them.
const retired_commands = [_]Retirement{
    .{ .name = "invoke", .message = "`mylang invoke` was retired with host functions (S5-D5); call the function from a `main` or a test" },
    .{ .name = "emit-qbe", .message = qbe_emission },
    .{ .name = "emit-project-qbe", .message = qbe_emission },
    .{ .name = "emit-runtime", .message = "the prebuilt QBE runtime archive was retired (S5); a build compiles the new core's runtime once per machine" },
    .{ .name = "dump-bytecode", .message = "the bytecode IR was retired (S5); `mylang dump-ir FILE.my` prints the Core IR" },
    .{ .name = "compile-module", .message = module_build_retired },
    .{ .name = "link-modules", .message = module_build_retired },
    .{ .name = "native-test-graph", .message = "the old test runner's internal commands were retired (S5); `mylang test` runs tests on the new core" },
    .{ .name = "native-test-compile", .message = "the old test runner's internal commands were retired (S5); `mylang test` runs tests on the new core" },
    .{ .name = "bench-promote", .message = "benchmark promotion was dropped (S5-D6)" },
};

/// Flags any command refuses, before `--`.
const retired_flags = [_]Retirement{
    .{ .name = "--gc-runtime", .message = runtime_retired },
    .{ .name = "--gc-stress", .message = "the tracing collector and its stress mode were retired (S5-D4); drop the flag" },
    .{ .name = "--rc-runtime", .message = runtime_retired },
    .{ .name = "--legacy-sharing", .message = "legacy sharing was retired (S5-D4): value semantics is the only mode; drop the flag" },
    .{ .name = "--value-semantics", .message = "--value-semantics was retired (S5-D4): value semantics is always on; drop the flag" },
    .{ .name = "--library", .message = host_retired },
    .{ .name = "--provider", .message = host_retired },
    .{ .name = "--link-library", .message = host_retired },
    .{ .name = "--library-path", .message = host_retired },
    .{ .name = "--unchecked", .message = "--unchecked was removed: every build keeps overflow, cast and bounds checks so optimization never changes a program's result" },
    .{ .name = "--explain-ownership", .message = "--explain-ownership read the old back end's ownership lowering (retired, S5); `mylang build --stats` reports the new core's" },
};

/// Flags `mylang test` refuses that its new runner dropped (S5-D6).
const dropped_test_flags = [_][]const u8{
    "--pure-skip",        "--diagnostics",  "--max-calls",     "--bench",        "--bench-only",    "--bench-output",
    "--bench-baseline",   "--bench-policy", "--bench-profile", "--bench-warmup", "--bench-batches", "--bench-batch-ms",
    "--bench-timeout-ms",
};

/// `build` and `run` flags `mylang test` has no use for, with the reason (S5-B2).
const test_only_retired = [_]Retirement{
    .{ .name = "--report", .message = "`mylang test --report` printed the old runner's work report (retired, S5-B2); `--json` reports each test, `cached` for a replayed receipt" },
    .{ .name = "--timings", .message = "`mylang test --timings` was retired with the old runner (S5-B2); `mylang run FILE.my --timings` times one program" },
    .{ .name = "--explain-invalidation", .message = "--explain-invalidation compared the old back end's nine stage fingerprints (retired, S5-B2); `--json` reports each test, `cached` for a replayed receipt" },
};

/// Why `mylang test` refuses the flag `name` (its `--flag` part): a flag `build` and
/// `run` retire with a pointer `test` cannot follow says what `test` offers instead.
fn testRefusal(arg: []const u8) ?[]const u8 {
    const name = arg[0 .. std.mem.indexOfScalar(u8, arg, '=') orelse arg.len];
    for (test_only_retired) |retired| if (std.mem.eql(u8, name, retired.name)) return retired.message;
    return cli_args.unported(arg);
}

// spec-case: NEW-CORE-S5-RETIRED-1
test "test refusals point at what test offers, not at flags test also refuses" {
    try std.testing.expect(std.mem.indexOf(u8, testRefusal("--explain-invalidation").?, "`--json`") != null);
    try std.testing.expect(std.mem.indexOf(u8, testRefusal("--explain-invalidation").?, "`--report`") == null);
    try std.testing.expect(std.mem.indexOf(u8, testRefusal("--report").?, "`--json`") != null);
    try std.testing.expect(std.mem.indexOf(u8, testRefusal("--friction-log=x").?, "mylang check") != null);
    try std.testing.expect(testRefusal("--json") == null);
    try std.testing.expect(std.mem.indexOf(u8, cli_args.unported("--explain-invalidation").?, "`--report`") != null);
}

/// Run `args` (argv, the program name first) when it names a command of the new
/// core or a retired one, returning its exit status; null leaves it to the caller.
pub fn dispatch(init: std.process.Init, args: []const []const u8) Error!?u8 {
    if (args.len < 2) return null;
    const command = args[1];
    const rest = args[2..];
    const status =
